@startuml qnx_dispatch_engine_connection_callbacks_activity
title QnxDispatchEngine - native connection callbacks

start
:Server dispatch_handler invokes io_open or io_close_ocb\non the server runner's background thread;
if (Entry is io_open?) then (yes)
    :ResmgrHandleToServer(handle)\nRecover the server and its shared engine;
    :Lock the server's native attributes;
    :iofunc_open(ctp, msg, server attributes);
    if (Native open verification fails?) then (yes)
        :Unlock server attributes;
        :Return the native verification error;
        stop
    else (verified)
        :Invoke server.ProcessConnect(ctp, msg);
        note right
            Concrete server policy supplies this callback.
            It can call AttachConnection, which forwards
            the supplied server and connection OCB
            to iofunc_ocb_attach.
        end note
    endif
    :Unlock server attributes;
    :Return the ProcessConnect result to native dispatch;
    stop
else (io_close_ocb)
    :OcbToServer and OcbToConnection\nRecover the bound adapters and engine;
endif

:Attempt to deliver the stored select event;
:Lock the OCB's server attributes;
:iofunc_ocb_detach(ctp, ocb);
:Unlock server attributes;
:Invoke connection.ProcessDisconnect();
:Return EOK to native dispatch;
stop

legend
    Native requests use the server dispatch context's receive workspace.
    This native request path runs on the server worker,
    separately from the Start/Stop interface caller.
    Attribute-lock/unlock, event-delivery and detach results are unchecked.
    Other I/O callbacks and their guards are described in the callback table.
    Connection allocation and application policy belong to the server unit.
endlegend

@enduml
